body:
Reading a bit here and there, I understood that training on non-sequential blocks might be useful based on certain needs. So, I wanted to do a small test, but I couldn’t find a way to train on a series of non-contiguous block layers. I tried inserting the “range” of blocks, “start” and “end” in series, on separate numbered layers, and even when the block series was noted in the JSON file, in the adapter file uploaded to Hugging Face, I saw that the trained layers were only the last ones in the series listed in the JSON file. By tinkering with the code and modifying some classes, I managed to get what I wanted. The files I modified are:
```
@dataclass
class BlockRange:
    start: Optional[int] = None
    end: Optional[int] = None
    indices: Optional[List[int]] = None

    def get_blocks(self) -> List[int]:
        if self.indices:
            return self.indices
        if self.start is not None and self.end is not None:
            return list(range(self.start, self.end - 1))
```
This is from the training_spec.py file. Part of this:
```
def _construct_layers(
    block_spec: TransformerBlocks | SingleTransformerBlocks,
    blocks: list[JointTransformerBlock] | list[SingleTransformerBlock],
    block_prefix: str,
) -> dict:
    block_indices = block_spec.block_range.get_blocks()  # Uses the method with ()
    lora_layers = {}
    for idx in block_indices:
        if idx >= len(blocks):
            raise IndexError(f"Index {idx} out of bounds of the block list.")

        block = blocks[idx]
        for layer_type in block_spec.layer_types:
            original_layer = LoRALayers._get_nested_attr(block, layer_type)
            is_list = isinstance(original_layer, list)

            lora_layer = LoRALinear.from_linear(
                linear=original_layer[0] if is_list else original_layer,
                r=block_spec.lora_rank,
            )
            layer_path = f"{block_prefix}.{idx}.{layer_type}"

            lora_layers[layer_path] = [lora_layer] if is_list else lora_layer

    return lora_layers
```
This is from the lora_layers.py file.
The LoRA layers created on Hugging Face show the trained layers, and in the JSON file, this appears: 
```
"lora_layers": {
        "single_transformer_blocks": {
            "block_range": {
                "indices": [
                    0,
                    1,
                    7,
                    19,
                    20
                ]
            },
            "layer_types": [
                "proj_out"
            ],
            "lora_rank": 128
        }
    },
```
